{"content":[{"id":"7028273b","settings":{"content_width":"boxed","boxed_width":{"size":900,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"padding":{"top":0,"right":0,"bottom":0,"left":0,"unit":"px","isLinked":true}},"elements":[{"id":"5b808a33","settings":{"design_skin":"chapter","uf_eyebrow":"CHAPTER THREE \/ THE LONG WAY HOME","title":"A journey worth taking slowly.","title_tag":"h3","content_source":"editor","editor_content":"<p>The road leaves the coast just after the harbour. For a few miles the sea appears between the trees, then disappears behind the first low hills. We stop for coffee in a village with no sign of being in a hurry.<\/p><p>There is no fixed itinerary for the afternoon. A footpath follows the river, a handwritten notice points towards an old orchard, and somewhere beyond the ridge there is said to be a view worth the climb.<\/p><p>By evening, the map has acquired a few new folds. We have travelled less far than planned and seen more than expected. Sometimes that is the best kind of progress.<\/p>","collapsed_height":{"size":170,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"collapsed_height_mobile":{"size":190,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"initial_state":"folded","unfold_duration":420,"expand_label":"Continue reading","collapse_label":"Show less","trigger":"click","show_button":"yes","show_overlay":"yes","hide_trigger_if_fits":"yes","uf_accent":"#86452f","uf_focus":"#86452f","box_padding":{"top":42,"right":44,"bottom":42,"left":44,"unit":"px","isLinked":false},"box_padding_mobile":{"top":28,"right":24,"bottom":28,"left":24,"unit":"px","isLinked":false},"box_gap":{"size":22,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"title_typography_typography":"custom","title_typography_font_family":"Georgia","title_typography_font_size":{"size":42,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"title_typography_font_size_mobile":{"size":29,"unit":"px","sizes":[]},"title_typography_font_weight":"500","title_typography_line_height":{"size":1.12,"unit":"em","sizes":[]},"content_typography_typography":"custom","content_typography_font_family":"Arial","